Implemented in lib/App/karr/Cmd/Dashboard.pm (+ t/220-dashboard.t). Decisions
made where the spec left them open:

- Bar cap: 10 blocks max per entry (MAX_BAR_BLOCKS), not 20 -- chosen so
  several entries fit per row at an ordinary 80-column terminal (the whole
  point of "mehrspaltig"). Above the cap, segments are scaled down by
  largest-remainder / Hamilton apportionment so the bar always sums to
  exactly 10; the exact count is always printed after the bar as "(N)" so
  compression never loses information, only visual precision.

- Blocked visibility: a blocked card is pulled OUT of its status colour into
  its own trailing bar segment, always bold red (matching Board.pm's
  blocked:reason colour). The apportionment guarantees that segment never
  rounds away to zero when blocked > 0, even under heavy compression --
  that is the one category the bar promises stays visible without reading
  text.

- Empty board (0 open tasks): bar area is simply blank, "(0)" is the only
  marker. No separate placeholder glyph -- it reads as "calm" and is
  visually distinct from a no-board repo, which is grouped in its own dimmed
  section entirely.

- Terminal width: $ENV{COLUMNS} first (numeric, works headless/piped, makes
  this testable without a real pty), then `tput cols`, then 80. Deliberately
  NOT gated on -t STDOUT (unlike colour, which follows Board.pm's convention
  exactly) -- width detection and colour are separate concerns here.

- Grid layout: ls -C style (one shared cell width = widest entry across the
  whole grid), but filled row-major (left-to-right, top-to-bottom / reading
  order) rather than ls's own column-major fill, since this is meant to be
  read start to finish, not scanned by column. Names are left-padded to a
  shared width first so every bar starts at the same horizontal offset
  regardless of grid position.

- Sort order: alphabetical by repo basename -- predictable, and keeps tests
  and screen output deterministic. No --sort flag; spec didn't ask for one
  and it's easy to add later if wanted.

- No-board repos: listed in one dimmed "No board: a, b, c" line below the
  grid, never mixed into it. --hide-no-board drops it entirely (and drops
  the JSON no_board key too).

- A repo that errors while being probed (corrupt refs, unreadable ref) is
  treated the same as "no board" plus one line on STDERR naming it --
  mirrors Foundation's own "one broken board cannot stop the rest of the
  run", so one bad repo can't blank the whole dashboard.

- Symlinked directories are never followed anywhere in the recursive scan
  (loop safety), full stop -- including a symlink that happens to point
  straight at a repo. The explicitly named start PATH itself is exempt (an
  explicit argument is trusted).

